//
// A `const` refusal names the parameter the AUTHOR wrote, at every parameter type
// (loft#1250).
//
// A mutated `text` argument is promoted to a hidden `__tp_<name>` local so a rebind has a
// slot to write, and the promoted local INHERITS the const axis so the guard still fires on
// it.  What it does not inherit is a name a reader can find: `fn f(s: const text)` reported
// *"Cannot modify const parameter '__tp_s'"*, sending the reader to look for a variable that
// appears nowhere in their program.  Every other parameter type named its parameter
// correctly, so the one type with a hidden work variable was the one type that lied.
//
// `Variables::const_report_var` is the one home for the question — it maps a promoted local
// back to the parameter it came from and every other variable to itself — and it existed
// throughout, asked by ONE of the five sites that report a const modification.  The other
// four read the raw variable.  That is the shape QUALITY.md calls a restated predicate: the
// home is not wrong, it is simply not asked, and the site that does ask it is the one that
// happens to be right.
//
// The NOUN travels with the name for the same reason.  A promoted `__tp_` local is not
// marked an argument, so a report against it would also say "const variable" where the
// author wrote a parameter — the cells below assert the whole sentence, not the name alone.
//
// @falsified-at: 4c4a7a56 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret expectations FAIL/7 -> 7/7,
//   native exit 1 -> 0, native expectations FAIL/7 -> 7/7.  On that build the two `const text`
//   PARAMETER cells report `'__tp_s'`, so the suite does not accept the file.
// @falsified-at: 5dda4f6d — the same movement for the OTHER defect this file now covers: on
//   that build (loft#1250 fixed, loft#1252 not) it is `const_text_local_that_is_returned`
//   that fails, reporting a "const parameter" in a function with none.  Two refs because two
//   defects: a single one would leave half the file unfalsified, and the cells that match on
//   BOTH builds are what make each ref a control rather than a file that stopped compiling.
//   `FAIL/7` is the SUITE's verdict and deliberately not a count of matching cells — see
//   loft#1253, which is why this file is scored through `--tests` at all.
//
// Non-vacuity, measured rather than assumed, because a guard made of expected failures is
// the shape that most easily passes while proving nothing: each of the five distinct
// substrings was replaced in turn with a word the compiler never prints, the suite failed
// six times out of six, and restoring each returned it to green.  It earned itself: the
// loft#1252 cell was VACUOUS on the first pass — it declared the same substring as the cell
// above it, and the suite matches the UNION of every round, so either annotation consumed
// either diagnostic.  Renaming its local to `r` is what made the two cells distinguishable.
//   HOLDS: assertions, leak, panic, free-refusal are equal on both trees. Falsify prints only
//   the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean, so each of those is clean on both.
//   The native side was not scored in this sweep.

struct B1250 { n: integer }

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 's'
fn const_text_parameter_names_itself(s: const text) -> text {
  s += "!";
  s
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 's'
fn const_text_parameter_names_itself_on_a_rebind(s: const text) -> text {
  s = "z";
  s
}

// The three that were already right, kept as the control: they share the diagnostic and not
// the promotion, so a change that reached the message rather than the name moves them too.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'a'
fn const_integer_parameter(a: const integer) -> integer {
  a += 1;
  a
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'v'
fn const_vector_parameter(v: const vector<integer>) {
  v += [1];
}

// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const parameter 'b'
fn const_struct_parameter(b: const B1250) {
  b.n = 9;
}

// A const LOCAL says "const variable" — the other half of the noun, and the cell that fails
// if `const_report_var` is applied where it does not belong.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const variable 'u'
fn const_text_local() -> integer {
  const u: text = "abc";
  u = "z";
  5
}

// …and it still says "const variable" when the local IS the function's returned text, which
// is the shape that did not (loft#1252).  A heap-typed local supplying the return value is
// promoted by `ref_return` to the hidden destination parameter, so it occupies an argument
// slot, and `const_kind` read that slot rather than the declaration: this function has no
// parameters and was told it had a const one.  The PROMOTION is visible in the type — the
// promoted local is a `RefVar` and a declared parameter is its bare type — which separates
// them without splitting the `argument` flag that 141 sites read and the IR schema persists.
//
// The pair is what makes it a guard rather than one cell: the two differ only in whether the
// local is returned, so a fix that reached the noun by any coarser route moves both.
// The local is named `r` and not `u` ON PURPOSE.  Written with the same name as the cell
// above, the two declare the IDENTICAL substring, and the suite matches against the union of
// every round — so either annotation consumes either diagnostic and corrupting one of them
// leaves the file green.  Measured: this cell was VACUOUS until it was renamed, and the
// non-vacuity check is what said so rather than a reading of it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: Cannot modify const variable 'r'
fn const_text_local_that_is_returned() -> text {
  const r: text = "abc";
  r = "z";
  r
}

// The `&` half of the discriminator is NOT a cell here, deliberately.  `const &τ` does not
// parse — `&` opts a binding INTO write-through and `const` opts it out, so the two never
// appear together in a signature, and that is what makes a `RefVar` at a const diagnostic
// always a promotion.  Written as an `@EXPECT_ERROR` cell it desyncs the parser into three
// cascading errors and takes the whole FILE with it, which would cost the six cells above to
// pin one fact about a spelling nobody writes.  It lives in `const_kind`'s doc-comment
// instead, where the code that depends on it is.
